  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "Let $V=C[0,3]$ be the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,3],$ equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n\u27e8f(t),g(t)\u27e9={\\int }_{0}^{3}f(t)g(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nGiven the functions $f(t)=t$ and $g(t)={e}^{t},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $\u27e8f(t),g(t)\u27e9=2{e}^{3}+1$\n- $\u27e8f(t),g(t)\u27e9=1$\n- $f(t)\u22a5g(t)$ with respect to the inner product $\u27e8\\cdot ,\\cdot \u27e9$ in $V$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we need to evaluate \n\n$$\n\u27e8f(t),g(t)\u27e9={\\int }_{0}^{3}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & \u27f9 & {u}^{\u2032}=1, \\\\ {v}^{\u2032}={e}^{t} & \u27f9 & v=\\int {v}^{\u2032}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, applying the integration by parts formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3}t{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{3}-{\\int }_{0}^{3}v{u}^{\u2032}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t{e}^{t}){|}_{0}^{3}-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(3{e}^{3}-0{e}^{0})-{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =3{e}^{3}-({e}^{3}-1) \\\\  & =2{e}^{3}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now examine the statements:\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. This is as a result of the above calculations.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the inner product is not zero, our vectors are not orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
